          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is high blood pressure, and how is it different from one high reading?",
          "answer": "High blood pressure, also known as hypertension, is defined as persistently elevated pressure in the arteries. It is measured with two numbers: systolic pressure (when the heart contracts) over diastolic pressure (when the heart is at rest between beats). These pressures are measured in millimeters of mercury (mmHg). The key aspect of hypertension is that it is sustained over time, rather than being based on a single high reading. Blood pressure can vary due to several factors, including activity, stress, and time of day. Therefore, a single high reading does not mean someone has hypertension; diagnosis requires repeated measurements (source: General clinical reference on Hypertension).\n\nA single high reading may indicate an elevated blood pressure at that moment, which can be influenced by various temporary factors like stress or caffeine. Since hypertension is typically without symptoms, it is often found incidentally during routine health screenings (source: General clinical reference on Hypertension).\n\nWhat I could not cover: specific causes of high blood pressure, detailed management strategies, or lifestyle recommendations related to hypertension.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
